Package: gpppSampler
Type: Package
Title: Gaussian Process Power Prior MCMC Sampler
Version: 0.5.1
Authors@R:
    person("Claire", "Zhu", email = "clairesunzhu@gmail.com",
           role = c("aut", "cre"))
Description: Implements a partially-collapsed Metropolis-within-Gibbs sampler
    for the Gaussian Process Power Prior (GPPP) model. For continuous and
    time-to-event outcomes the noise precision tau is unknown and the
    discount a0 acts on the complete historical likelihood (the full power
    prior); kernel hyperparameters and a0 are updated collapsed (f*
    marginalized out, with the tau-integrated normalizing constant evaluated
    by fixed-node quadrature), while the latent GP vector f* is drawn jointly
    each iteration to support a conjugate gamma update of tau. For binary
    outcomes the model uses a logit link with Polya-Gamma augmentation: a0
    discounts the true observed-data logistic likelihood (not the
    complete-data augmented-latent likelihood, a different, non-equivalent
    prior); kernel hyperparameters are updated collapsed over the latent GP
    vector given the current Polya-Gamma weights, and a0 is updated
    conditional on the latent vector using a Laplace-approximated
    normalizing constant, since the augmentation gives exact latent draws
    but not this normalizer. Time-to-event (log-normal AFT) outcomes are
    handled via data augmentation of censored historical times
    (Section 2.4); the discounted censoring-survival term has the same
    normalizing-constant issue as the binary case and is not yet addressed.
    Kernels include squared exponential, Matern 5/2, and
    linear, all with ARD length-scales. Both fixed and random discounting
    parameter a0 are supported. The profiled hot paths for the continuous/tte
    (unknown-tau) case with a supported kernel -- kernel matrix construction,
    collapsed marginal likelihood evaluation, and the a0 update's
    tau-integrated quadrature -- are implemented in C++ via Rcpp and
    RcppArmadillo; the binary-outcome path remains pure R. Statistical
    derivations follow the working paper "Gaussian Process Power Prior"
    (Zhu, 2026).
